            <ArticleTitle>The role of interventional radiology in complications after paediatric liver transplantation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>825-829</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.7196/samj.8619</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Liver transplantation has become an established treatment in both adults and children for end-stage liver disease, acute hepatic failure and certain liver tumours. There is a significant rate of complications after all forms of liver transplantation. The interventional radiologist plays a critical role in the diagnosis and treatment of these complications. The use of image-guided, minimally invasive procedures reduces the need for surgical revision or retransplantation, and improves graft and patient survival rate. This article reviews some of the most common vascular and non-vascular complications after paediatric liver transplantation, and the interventional radiology techniques used to diagnose and treat them.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
